sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch05-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Traditional fractal geometry does not consider interactions between elements, which is why all patterns it produces are purely nested; allowing replacements to depend on neighbors on a grid readily yields complicated, non-nested patterns.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Fractal geometry misses complexity by excluding interactions between elements";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 5, “Two Dimensions and Beyond”; section “Substitution Systems and Fractals” (from p. 187), p. 190. Scope: fractal geometry and two-dimensional neighbor-dependent substitution systems"
}
